{{!-- Who is setting out, where they are bound, and which map is showing.

      SHARED BY BOTH SURFACES. The route step draws these above its map; the "See the whole map"
      window draws the same controls above its viewport, which is what makes the popout a peer of
      the panel rather than a picture of it. Context is the journey object from
      ExpeditionDialog._buildJourney.

      Every control is bound once, in that dialog's activateListeners, and the window forwards its
      own clicks to the very same methods — so "change the origin" cannot come to mean two
      different things depending on which window you did it in. --}}
<div class="stonetop-journey-picks">
  <label class="stonetop-journey-picklab">
    Setting out from
    <select class="stonetop-journey-origin">
      {{#each journey.originOptions}}
      <option value="{{slug}}"{{#if selected}} selected{{/if}}>{{name}}</option>
      {{/each}}
    </select>
  </label>
  {{#if journey.hasDestination}}
  <span class="stonetop-journey-bound">bound for <strong>{{journey.destination.name}}</strong></span>
  <button type="button" class="stonetop-journey-clear" data-tooltip="Clear the destination">
    <i class="fas fa-xmark"></i>
  </button>
  {{else}}
  <span class="stonetop-journey-bound stonetop-journey-bound--empty">Tap a place to set the destination.</span>
  {{/if}}
  {{!-- Put the way they go onto the table's own map. It sits with the DESTINATION rather than with
        the map tabs because it is an act on the journey, not a choice of picture: the scene it
        draws on is the one the reader is looking at, which need not be the map showing here.

        GM-only, and absent rather than disabled for a player: it writes a Scene flag, which a
        player cannot do, and a control that is visibly there and refuses on click is worse than
        one that never offered. Players still SEE the line, because the flag broadcasts. Built only
        once a destination is picked, for the same reason. See utils/scene-route.js. --}}
  {{#if journey.scene}}
  <button type="button" class="stonetop-journey-to-scene{{#if journey.scene.showing}} is-showing{{/if}}"
          data-tooltip="{{journey.scene.tooltip}}">
    <i class="fas {{journey.scene.icon}}"></i> {{journey.scene.label}}
  </button>
  {{/if}}
</div>

{{!-- Lay the way out by hand instead of by the table (module/utils/custom-route.js).

      ITS OWN ROW, between the picks and the map tabs, because it belongs to both and crowds
      either: it changes what a click on the picture MEANS, and it changes what the readout below
      is about. The row is also where the three gestures are taught. They have to be taught
      somewhere — nothing on a map advertises that shift adds a leg — and a line of text that is
      only there while the mode is on costs nothing the rest of the time.

      The checkbox is GM-only (`canDraw`), like the scene and site buttons beside it and for the
      same reason: drawing writes the trip into a world setting, which a player cannot do. The row
      itself is not, so a player reading the route step still sees that this way was drawn by
      hand rather than worked out. --}}
{{#if journey.custom.canDraw}}
<div class="stonetop-journey-draw{{#if journey.custom.on}} is-on{{/if}}">
  <label class="stonetop-journey-drawlab" data-tooltip="{{journey.custom.tooltip}}">
    <input type="checkbox" class="stonetop-journey-custom"{{#if journey.custom.on}} checked{{/if}}>
    {{journey.custom.label}}
  </label>
  {{#if journey.custom.on}}
  <span class="stonetop-journey-drawhint">{{journey.custom.hint}}</span>
  {{#if journey.custom.count}}
  <button type="button" class="stonetop-journey-clear-drawn" data-tooltip="{{journey.custom.clearTip}}">
    <i class="fas fa-rotate-left"></i> {{journey.custom.clearLabel}}
  </button>
  {{/if}}
  {{/if}}
</div>
{{else if journey.route.drawn}}
{{!-- A player, looking at a way somebody drew. No control, but the line on this map is not the one
      the travel table would have drawn and the times beside it are measured rather than printed,
      so it says so. Its own sentence, not the GM's: the three gestures are no use to a reader who
      cannot make them. --}}
<div class="stonetop-journey-draw is-on">
  <span class="stonetop-journey-drawhint">{{journey.custom.drawnNote}}</span>
</div>
{{/if}}

{{!-- The map row: which picture, and what the GM can do to it.

      The tablist is its own element INSIDE the row rather than being the row, because a tablist
      may hold tabs and nothing else. The button beside it is not a tab (it changes no panel and
      selects nothing), so a reader told "tab 3 of 3" about it would be told something untrue. --}}
<div class="stonetop-journey-tiers">
  <div class="stonetop-journey-tierlist" role="tablist" aria-label="Map">
    {{#each journey.tiers}}
    <button type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="{{isActive}}" data-tier="{{slug}}"
            class="stonetop-journey-tier{{#if isActive}} is-active{{/if}}">
      {{name}}{{#unless hasMap}} <i class="fas fa-image-slash" data-tooltip="Not imported"></i>{{/unless}}
    </button>
    {{/each}}
  </div>
  {{!-- Put one of the GM's own written-up sites on this map, or write a new one and put THAT on it
        (module/sites/place-site-on-map.js). It rides the end of the map-tab row rather than the
        row of picks above, because what it adds is a mark on THIS picture: the chooser is followed
        by a click on the map showing here, so the control belongs beside the tabs that decide
        which map that is.

        GM-only, and absent rather than disabled for a player, exactly as the scene button above
        is: a site is prep filed in a journal players cannot see or write. It is also built only
        once there is a map on screen, since a placement is a point on a picture. --}}
  {{#if journey.placeSite}}
  {{!-- SITE_ICON, the mark a site wears in a LIST (module/sites/place-site-on-map.js) and the one
        the chooser this button opens puts beside every row in it. NOT the standing stone a placed
        site wears as a pin on the picture below: that one is drawn at 18px, where this one does
        not read. One idea, two sizes, two drawings -- and this control belongs with the list. --}}
  <button type="button" class="stonetop-journey-place-site" data-tooltip="{{journey.placeSite.tooltip}}">
    <i class="fas fa-mountain-sun"></i> {{journey.placeSite.label}}
  </button>
  {{/if}}
</div>
